not_worth_it.reason:
The low-latency (rtvc) session refuses all four with kVTPropertyNotSupportedErr (-12900), measured here.

Switching to standard rate control to use them gains 1.4-2.7 ms dwell p50 at 1080p (H.265 with speed: 3.6 ms against 6.3 ms), but:
- PSNR falls 5.7-13.7 dB, for example 29.96 against 43.65 dB for H.265 at 1080p;
- p95 is worse: 9.6-16 ms against 6.8-8.6 ms;
- standard rate control fits neither rate model (prior L run).

Supported under low latency and honoured, with zero drops measured. However:
- it must be set on every frame and turns off VideoToolbox's rate control and DataRateLimits;
- fixed-QP encodes measured 6.7-10.8 ms dwell p50 at 1080p, against 5-6 ms under low-latency rate control;
- a host QP loop lags the pipelined encoder by 1-4 frames, with no content lookahead.

The net effect is not established and the measured latency is worse.

reviews.effect_assessment:
The effect is real when ScreenCaptureKit's buffers are unpadded:
- 1.3-2.6 ms lower encoder dwell at 1440p- and 4K-class scaled sizes at 60 fps;
- at 120 fps on large scaled sizes, 10-37 ms lower dwell at the same frame rate, since a synchronous encode never queues;
- unclear at 1080p-class sizes.

It is zero if ScreenCaptureKit already pads its surfaces, which is device-only. It applies only to scaled sizes; native 1:1 sizes need R2-vt-padded-capture-sps-crop.

The aspect distortion is at most about 0.8%, for example 1920x1072 from 16:9.

