research.summary:
The pointer path today, step by step. The viewer sends one pointer move per Flutter event. Listener.onPointerHover/Move (ui/lib/src/pages/remote/viewer/remote_surface.dart:244-253) calls RemoteInput._moveTo (remote_input.dart:542-547), which calls DesktopStore.send. That is a 4 ms leading-edge gate whose trailing flush is a Dart Timer (desktop_store.dart:64, 2586-2600, 2641-2647). The move then goes through the synchronous FFI op `input` on the UI isolate, encoded as JSON both ways (ui/lib/src/ffi/desktop_api.dart:925-961, ui/native/pb_mapper_ffi/src/desktop/viewer.rs:582-662). Rust stamps it into Outbound, which replaces a queued tail move (crates/pb-mapper-desktop/src/viewer/outbound.rs:227-289). The session's writer, one tokio task that races the reader (viewer/session.rs:211-215, 497-549), writes a TLS record onto the loopback TCP pair. The tunnel worker carries it over QUIC or the relay. On the host, the publisher writes it to a loopback socket. B-recv parses it, pushes it onto the bounded lane and wakes thread C (session.rs:425-429, lane.rs:44-59). Thread C drains the lane before and after its single capture call and injects each event (worker.rs:833-856, worker/control.rs:305-420); through the helper, each event is one pipe round trip (oop/client.rs:352-361).

Hops and transport. After the UI thread there are two task hops on the viewer and two or three on the host, all event-driven; the only timer in the path is Dart's trailing gate. Input uses no datagrams: it rides the viewer-to-host direction of the session's one ordered TLS stream. That direction carries only small control messages, as airhand's reliable control stream does (crates/airhand-proto/src/session.rs:9-17, 600-606).

Cursor. The controller sees its own OS pointer, so position has zero latency. The OS pointer takes the host's shape for 13 named kinds. Every other shape is a Flutter-painted bitmap under a hidden pointer (remote_cursor_layer.dart:46-73, 203-251). By contrast, airhand turns every host shape into a native CSS cursor (client/src/lib/session.ts:788-836). The host samples position and shape on a fixed 16 ms clock (worker/cursor.rs:11, 21-37) and writes them ahead of queued video.

Injection. macOS posts one CGEvent per event (platform/macos/input.rs:122-126, 275-329). Windows uses SetCursorPos for moves and enigo SendInput for buttons, wheel and keys (platform/windows/input.rs:53-84). An injected input makes DXGI capture hot and polled every 2 ms (worker/capture.rs:157-164).

Against airhand, the core path is already equivalent or better: a 4 ms gate against airhand's 8 ms, presentation-gated freshness in place of INPUT_MAX_AGE, and a measured 3.1-3.4 ms mean send-to-observed move through the installed helper (docs/validation/desktop-925-installed-input.json). The gaps are at the edges:
- custom pointer shapes are painted by Flutter rather than shown as native cursors;
- the Windows viewer's Dart timers run on the 15.6 ms tick;
- scrolling is sent in whole notches only;
- Windows moves use SetCursorPos, which produces no raw input and does not reset the idle timer, and the Windows host does not keep the display awake;
- shape changes wait for the 16 ms clock;
- there is no relative pointer or pointer-lock mode.

