reviews.effect_assessment:
Real for viewers whose consumer is slower than the host's rate:
- Linux viewers on software decode (avdec plus CPU videoconvert to RGBA), especially at 1080p and above 60 fps or on weak CPUs;
- Windows viewers on software Media Foundation, as in VMs;
- 4K at 120 fps on weak integrated GPUs.

Today such a consumer overshoots about every 5-6 s. Most overshoots end in an age drop: a frozen picture until an IDR arrives (round trip plus encode plus IDR transfer, seconds on a 1 Mbit/s link) and an IDR bandwidth spike. The mean pace is 12.4 for a 14.9 fps consumer.

After the change (estimates):
- no drops in steady state;
- the pace holds at 13-14, about 10-13% more frames;
- an occasional probe is reverted before any drop.
With the corrected raise rule, recovery after a transient slowdown is no slower than today and somewhat faster.

Consumers that keep up never build a queue, so they are unaffected.

